      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\nDocument-level:\n- number: R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.06\n- scheme: A\n- project_number: not explicitly mentioned (CZ1 maybe? Actually the number format is R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.06; project number not given separately)\n- page_total: 7\n- member_state: Czech Republic\n- date_issued: 11 November 2024 → 2024-11-11\n- oiml_issuer_id: not given explicitly... The V 3112 is a logo mark. Not clearly an issuer ID. Skip.\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: Czech Metrology Institute\n- address_lines: [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"]\n- person_responsible: Jan Kalandra (on page 1). But signed by RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body. The schema has person_responsible and person_title. Page 1 says \"Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\". The signature block says RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body. I'll use person_responsible: Jan Kalandra. Hmm, but the schema person_title might refer to the person_responsible's title, which isn't given. I could include the signer... The schema has only one person fields. I'll use Jan Kalandra as person_responsible (as labeled). Person title not given for him. Maybe mention Klenovský? No — don't fabricate. Just keep Jan Kalandra.\n\nApplicants:\n- name: JANZ - Contagem e Gestao de Fluídos, S.A. (the name field weirdly includes \"Obergrundstrasse 119\" appended, but that's likely OCR bleed; Address: Avenida Infante D. Henrique, 288, 1950-421 Lisboa, PT). The \"Obergrundstrasse 119\" looks like artifact. I'll put name: \"JANZ - Contagem e Gestao de Fluídos, S.A.\" and address_lines: [\"Avenida Infante D. Henrique, 288\", \"1950-421 Lisboa\", \"PT\"] (or keep as single line). I'll format address lines: [\"Avenida Infante D. Henrique, 288, 1950-421 Lisboa, PT\"].\n\nManufacturers: same as applicant.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" — schema examples list categories; water meter fits. Use \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: [\"Smartio\"]\n- module_designation: \"-\" → Not applicable (the designation of module is \"-\", so \"Not applicable\")\n- description: the description text.\n\nmodel_family: The cert covers variants by nominal diameter DN15 and DN20, and by Q3 and R ratio (Q3/Q1). The flowrate tables give many variants. So model_family models could be combos: DN15 × Q3 ∈ {1.6, 2.5} × R ∈ {40...1000}, DN20 × Q3 ∈ {2.5, 4.0} × R ∈ {40...1000}.\n\nLet me enumerate the tables:\n\nDN15 table 1 (R = 40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125):\nQ3/Q1 pairs (each spans 2 columns):\n- R40: Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R40: Q1=0.063, Q2=0.100, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R50: Q1=0.032, Q2=0.051, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R50: Q1=0.050, Q2=0.080, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R63: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.041, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R63: Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R80: Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R80: Q1=0.031, Q2=0.050, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R100: Q1=0.016, Q2=0.026, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R100: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R125: Q1=0.013, Q2=0.021, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R125: Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n\nDN15 table 2 (R = 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500):\n- R160: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R160: Q1=0.016, Q2=0.025, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R200: Q1=0.008, Q2=0.013, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R200: Q1=0.013, Q2=0.020, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R250: Q1=0.006, Q2=0.010, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R250: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R315: Q1=0.005, Q2=0.008, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R315: Q1=0.008, Q2=0.013, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R400: Q1=0.004, Q2=0.006, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R400: Q1=0.006, Q2=0.010, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R500: Q1=0.003, Q2=0.005, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R500: Q1=0.005, Q2=0.008, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n\nDN15 table 3 (R = 630, 800, 1000):\n- R630: Q1=0.0025, Q2=0.004, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R630: Q1=0.004, Q2=0.006, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R800: Q1=0.003, Q2=0.005, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R1000: Q1=0.0025, Q2=0.004, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n\nNote: table 3 R800 and R1000 only for Q3=2.5. Interesting: R800 Q1=0.003? For Q3=2.5, Q1 = Q3/R = 2.5/800 = 0.003125 ≈ 0.003. R1000: 2.5/1000 = 0.0025. OK.\n\nDN20 table 1 (R = 40...125):\n- R40: Q1=0.063, Q2=0.100, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R40: Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n- R50: Q1=0.050, Q2=0.080, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R50: Q1=0.080, Q2=0.128, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n- R63: Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R63: Q1=0.064, Q2=0.102, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n- R80: Q1=0.031, Q2=0.050, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R80: Q1=0.050, Q2=0.080, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n- R100: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R100: Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n- R125: Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R125: Q1=0.032, Q2=0.051, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n\nDN20 table 2 (R = 160...500):\n- R160: Q1=0.016, Q2=0.025, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R160: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n- R200: Q1=0.013, Q2=0.020, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R200: Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n- R250: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R250: Q1=0.016, Q2=0.026, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n- R315: Q1=0.008, Q2=0.013, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R315: Q1=0.013, Q2=0.020, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n- R400: Q1=0.006, Q2=0.010, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R400: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n- R500: Q1=0.005, Q2=0.008, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R500: Q1=0.008, Q2=0.013, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n\nDN20 table 3 (R = 630, 800, 1000):\n- R630: Q1=0.004, Q2=0.006, Q3=2.500, Q4=3.125\n- R630: Q1=0.006, Q2=0.010, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n- R800: Q1=0.005, Q2=0.008, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n- R1000: Q1=0.004, Q2=0.006, Q3=4.00, Q4=5.00\n\nSo total variants: DN15: 12 + 12 + 4 = 28; DN20: 12 + 12 + 4 = 28. Total 56 variants. That's a lot but doable.\n\nI'll produce model_family with models having model_id like \"Smartio DN15 Q3=1.6 R=40\" etc. And matrix_tables preserving the 6 tables. And model_level characteristics for q1, q2, q3, q4 per model.\n\nHmm — 56 models × 4 attributes = 224 entries in model_level values arrays (4 attributes × 56 values). That's large but correct. Alternatively, use matrix_tables to hold the data and keep model_level... The R137 example populates both. Let me do both but it will be long. Actually, the matrix tables fully capture the data. The instructions say EXTRACT as matrix_tables AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level. I'll follow that.\n\nGiven the volume, maybe I can populate model_level with q1, q2, q3, q4 for all 56 models. That's 4 attributes × 56 values each. Long but acceptable.\n\nActually, let me think about whether the DN15/DN20 differences beyond flowrates matter:\n- Q3/Q1 characteristic: \"≤ 1000 1 for Q3=2.5m3/h, ≤ 630 1 for Q3=1.6m3/h\" for DN15; \"≤ 1000 for Q3=4m3/h, ≤630 for Q3=2,5m3/h\" for DN20. Interesting: the photo shows R=1600 for DN25 but tables max 1000. The table data: DN15 has R up to 1000 but only for Q3=2.5; R630 for both Q3=1.6 and 2.5. DN20: R630 for both Q3 2.5 and 4.0; R800/1000 only Q3=4.0.\n\n- Pressure loss class: DN15: Q3 1.6 Δp10, Q3 2.5 Δp16; DN20: Q3 2.5 Δp16, Q3 4.0 Δp25. This is per nominal-diameter config.\n- Connection type: DN15: NPSM or G type ¾\", 7/8\", 1\"; DN20: NPSM or G type 7/8\", 1\", 1 1/4\".\n- Length: ≥105mm both.\n\nSo there is a config axis \"nominal_diameter\" (DN15 vs DN20) for pressure loss class and connection type. I can use config_level for those, or model_level with model ids being the full variants. Given the flowrate tables define model variants by DN+Q3+R, the connection type and pressure loss class vary only by DN. I could put them in config_level with axis \"nominal_diameter\".\n\nLet me structure:\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: nominal_diameter, values: [{condition: \"DN15\", value: \"Q3=1.6: Δp10; Q3=2.5: Δp16\"}, {condition: \"DN20\", value: \"Q3=2.5: Δp16; Q3=4.0: Δp25\"}]. Hmm, value should be structured: {value, unit_symbol, ...}. The schema for config_level says values[]: {condition, value}. Following rule 5, values follow structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol}. The value here is a string like \"Δp10 (Q3=1.6), Δp16 (Q3=2.5)\". OK.\n\n- attribute: connection_type, axis: nominal_diameter, values: [{condition: \"DN15\", value: \"NPSM or G type 3/4\\\", 7/8\\\", 1\\\"\"}, {condition: \"DN20\", value: \"NPSM or G type 7/8\\\", 1\\\", 1 1/4\\\"\"}].\n\nAlso Q3/Q1 ratio availability: attribute: available_ratio_q3_q1, axis: nominal_diameter_and_q3. Could put: DN15: up to 630 for Q3=1.6, up to 1000 for Q3=2.5; DN20: up to 630 for Q3=2.5, up to 1000 for Q3=4.0. This is derivable from tables; I'll add as config_level entries maybe. Might be redundant. I'll include one entry: attribute: \"max_ratio_q3_q1\", axis: \"nominal_diameter_and_q3\": values [{condition \"DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h\", value 630}, {condition \"DN15, Q3=2.5 m³/h\", value 1000}, {condition \"DN20, Q3=2.5 m³/h\", value 630}, {condition \"DN20, Q3=4.0 m³/h\", value 1000}]. The \"1\" suffix in \"≤ 1000 1\" — hmm \"1)\" footnote? Actually footnote 1 is defined at bottom of page 5: \"¹ The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen according to paragraph 4.1.4 of OIML R 49-1:2013\". The \"1\" after 1000 and 630 likely refers to footnote 1. Actually in markdown it's \"≤ 1000 1 for Q3=2.5m3/h\" — the superscript 1 marker. So footnote marker \"1\" applies. Wait but the footnote text is about ratio selection, and the marked rows are Q3/Q1 and power supply etc. The footnote ¹ markers appear on \"Type (battery, mains AC, mains DC)¹\", \"U max (V)¹\", \"U min (V)¹\", \"Frequency¹\", \"Software version¹\" — all with footnote 1. And Q3/Q1 row also has the 1 markers. OK.\n\nFootnotes: marker \"1)\" or \"1\"? Text: \"¹ The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen according to paragraph 4.1.4 of OIML R 49-1:2013\". I'll record marker \"1\", text as given.\n\nType-level characteristics:\n- measuring_principle: \"ultrasonic\"\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- maximum_permissible_error_lower_zone: ±5 % → value: -5? Hmm, MPE ±5% → value: 5, unit: \"%\", attribute mpe_lower_zone... The value is ±5. I'd write value: 5 with note? Schema: value can be number or string. \"±5\" as string is not great. I could use value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\", and attribute \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_zone_mpel\". ±5% means magnitude. I'll use 5. Hmm, but \"±\" matters. Alternatively value: {min: -5, max: 5}? That's a range of permissible error. I think value: 5 with unit \"%\" is fine; attribute name includes mpe. Actually let me keep it simple: value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\". Hmm, could also do string \"±5\". Rule says numbers not strings. Use 5.\n- temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_pressure_class / maximum_admissible_pressure_class: \"MAP 16\"\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: 50, unit: °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, unit: MPa\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- indicating_range_testing_mode: 9999, unit \"m³\"; indicating_range_user_mode: 999999 m³. Given \"9 999 / 999 999\" → testing 9999, user 999999. Two attributes.\n- resolution_indicating_device_testing_mode: 0.00001, unit \"m³\"; resolution_indicating_device_user_mode: 0.001 m³.\n- resolution_device_rapid_testing: 100 pulse/dm³ → value 100, unit \"pulse/dm³\".\n- resolution_indicating_device_rapid_testing: 0.000001 m³.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- flow_conditioner_required: false / \"No\"\n- mounting: \"-\" → omit or null. Omit.\n- orientation: \"any\"\n- minimum_straight_length_inlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- length: ≥105 mm → {min: 105, max: null}, unit mm.\n- reverse_flow: \"Designed to measure\"\n- mechanical_environment_class: \"B, O\" → could be list [\"B\",\"O\"]. Value string \"B, O\". Environmental classification (B or O). I'll use value \"B, O\" string? It's a class enumeration; string is fine.\n- electromagnetic_environment_class: \"E2\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: -25 to 70 °C → {min: -25, max: 70}, unit \"°C\".\n- power_supply_type: \"Non replaceable battery\" (footnote 1)\n- power_supply_voltage_max: 3.6 V (U max); power_supply_voltage_min: 1.9 V (U min). Battery nominal 3.6V per description. I'll record umax=3.6, umin=1.9.\n- power_supply_frequency: \"-\" → omit (empty).\n- minimum_battery_lifetime: 16 years → value 16, unit \"years\" (or \"a\"). Use \"years\".\n- software_version: [\"0.6.28\", \"1.0.1\"] → value as string \"0.6.28, 1.0.1\" or list. Value can be string. I'll use [\"0.6.28\",\"1.0.1\"]? The schema shows value as number|object|string. A list isn't in shape; use string \"0.6.28, 1.0.1\". Hmm, or two? I'll use string.\n- crc_checksum: \"0x854EBACF, 0x13372073, 0x3A681C19, 0x1d901C40\" string.\n- software_type: \"P\" (embedded, built-for-purpose type P), extensions: I1, S, D. Could add characteristics: software_extensions: \"I1, S, D\" or separate. I'll add \"software_requirements\": \"Specific requirements for embedded software for built-for-purpose measuring instrument (type P)\" and \"software_extensions\": [\"I1: Water meters\", \"S: Software separation\", \"D: Download of Legally Relevant Software\"] as string. Maybe a single attribute \"software_extensions\": \"I1 (Water meters), S (Software separation), D (Download of Legally Relevant Software)\". OK.\n- eut_testing_category: \"Ultrasonic water meters, Coriolis water meters, fluidic water meters\"\n- eut_case: \"B\"\n- q2_q1_ratio: 1.6\n- q3_q4_ratio: 1.25\n- volume_resolution: 0.00001 m³ (from description) — already covered by resolution testing mode.\n- power supply battery 3.6V nominal — description says powered mains battery 3.6V (typo for \"powered by mains battery\"?). Umax 3.6 covers it.\n\nMarking: temperature class T50, MAP16, Δp classes, U0 D0, E2, B or O — covered.\n\nmodel_level characteristics: q1, q2, q3, q4 per model, and ratio (q3_q1) per model. Model ids: \"Smartio DN15 Q3=1.6 R=40\" etc. Let me generate the list of 56 models.\n\nDN15 (nominal_diameter 15):\nQ3=1.6:\n- R40: q1 0.040, q2 0.064, q4 2.000\n- R50: q1 0.032, q2 0.051\n- R63: q1 0.025, q2 0.041\n- R80: q1 0.020, q2 0.032\n- R100: q1 0.016, q2 0.026\n- R125: q1 0.013, q2 0.021\n- R160: q1 0.010, q2 0.016\n- R200: q1 0.008, q2 0.013\n- R250: q1 0.006, q2 0.010\n- R315: q1 0.005, q2 0.008\n- R400: q1 0.004, q2 0.006\n- R500: q1 0.003, q2 0.005\n- R630: q1 0.0025, q2 0.004\nQ3=2.5:\n- R40: 0.063/0.100\n- R50: 0.050/0.080\n- R63: 0.040/0.064\n- R80: 0.031/0.050\n- R100: 0.025/0.040\n- R125: 0.020/0.032\n- R160: 0.016/0.025\n- R200: 0.013/0.020\n- R250: 0.010/0.016\n- R315: 0.008/0.013\n- R400: 0.006/0.010\n- R500: 0.005/0.008\n- R630: 0.004/0.006\n- R800: 0.003/0.005\n- R1000: 0.0025/0.004\n\nDN15 count: 13 + 15 = 28. ✓\n\nDN20:\nQ3=2.5:\n- R40: 0.063/0.100\n- R50: 0.050/0.080\n- R63: 0.040/0.064\n- R80: 0.031/0.050\n- R100: 0.025/0.040\n- R125: 0.020/0.032\n- R160: 0.016/0.025\n- R200: 0.013/0.020\n- R250: 0.010/0.016\n- R315: 0.008/0.013\n- R400: 0.006/0.010\n- R500: 0.005/0.008\n- R630: 0.004/0.006\nQ3=4.0:\n- R40: 0.100/0.160\n- R50: 0.080/0.128\n- R63: 0.064/0.102\n- R80: 0.050/0.080\n- R100: 0.040/0.064\n- R125: 0.032/0.051\n- R160: 0.025/0.040\n- R200: 0.020/0.032\n- R250: 0.016/0.026\n- R315: 0.013/0.020\n- R400: 0.010/0.016\n- R500: 0.008/0.013\n- R630: 0.006/0.010\n- R800: 0.005/0.008\n- R1000: 0.004/0.006\n\nDN20 count: 13 + 15 = 28. ✓ Total 56.\n\nq4 = 1.25 × q3: DN15 Q3=1.6 → 2.000; Q3=2.5 → 3.125. DN20 Q3=2.5 → 3.125; Q3=4.0 → 5.00.\n\nq2 = 1.6 × q1 (given Q2/Q1 = 1.6). The table values: e.g., DN15 R40 Q3=1.6: Q2 = 0.064 = 1.6×0.040 ✓. Some values like DN20 R63 Q3=4.0: Q2=0.102 = 1.6×0.064=0.1024 ≈ 0.102 (rounded). Fine, use table values.\n\nModel ID format: \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R40\" or \"Smartio DN15 Q3 1.6 R 40\". I'll use \"Smartio DN15 Q3=1.6 R=40\". Consistent.\n\nThat's 56 model entries. Each with attributes maybe embedded? The schema for models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. I could put q1,q2,q3,q4 in each model's attributes AND in model_level. That's duplicative. The R137 example puts models as just model_id and uses model_level for values. I'll follow that: models with model_id only, and model_level for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio.\n\nmodel_level arrays: 5 attributes × 56 values = 280 value objects. That's big but let's do it. Actually maybe also nominal_diameter per model (15 or 20) — 56 more. Hmm. That could be attributes too. To manage size, I'll include nominal_diameter in model_level too? That's derivable from model_id. I'll skip nominal_diameter as separate attribute — it's encoded in the model_id. Actually adding it makes the data cleaner for the caller. Size concern: each value object ~ {\"model\": \"Smartio DN15 Q3=1.6 R=40\", \"value\": 0.04} — fine. 6 attributes × 56 = 336 objects. It's a lot of tokens but okay. Let me consider trimming: I'll include nominal_diameter, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1. That's thorough.\n\nHmm, output length constraints — this will be a very long JSON. Let's estimate: 56 models in model_family (56 short objects), 336 model_level value objects (~20 tokens each = 6700 tokens), 6 matrix tables with rows (28+28 rows each with 6 fields...). Wait matrix tables: I have 6 tables; rows total 28+28 = 56 rows across 6 tables. Each row: {nominal_diameter, ratio_q3_q1, q1, q2, q3, q4} ~ 40 tokens → 2240 tokens. Total maybe 12-15k tokens output. That's long but acceptable given the task demands completeness. Let me be efficient and avoid redundancy: The matrix_tables fully capture rows; model_level duplicates. The rules say to do both (R137 example). I'll comply but keep values compact.\n\nAlternative: reduce duplication by making matrix_tables the canonical and model_level minimal? The rules explicitly show both. I'll do both.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe combine the three DN15 tables into one matrix table with 28 rows (nominal_diameter=15), and the three DN20 tables into one with 28 rows. The certificate presents them as separate tables grouped by R range, but semantically it's one table per DN split across page breaks. Preserving structure: I could emit 6 matrix_tables matching the certificate's 6 tables, or 2 combined. I'll emit 6 tables to preserve fidelity (name: \"Basic metrological data (flowrates) – DN15, R 40–125\" etc.). Hmm, that adds name overhead ×6. Fine.\n\nActually to reduce output size, I'll emit 2 matrix tables (\"DN15 flowrates\", \"DN20 flowrates\") with 28 rows each. The description can note they were split across three sub-tables in the source. That's reasonable and more usable. Hmm, \"preserve multi-dim tables\" — combining related sub-tables is fine as they have identical columns. I'll do 6 to be safe? Let me weigh: 6 tables with headers repeated vs 2. Each table header ~ 40 tokens; overhead 4×40=160 tokens. Trivial. I'll do 6 to preserve fidelity to the source. Actually, wait — the column \"ratio\" name: \"q3_q1_ratio\". Rows keyed: {\"nominal_diameter\": 15, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 40, \"q1\": 0.040, \"q2\": 0.064, \"q3\": 1.6, \"q4\": 2.0}.\n\nNote: values like 0.040 → 0.04 in JSON (trailing zeros dropped). Acceptable; JSON numbers. 0.0025 fine. 1.600 → 1.6.\n\nTest reports:\n- id: \"0511-ER-V109-23\", date: 2024-11-11, pages: 25, role: type_evaluation_report\n- id: \"6015-PT-P5013-24\", pages: 201, role: test_report (date not given)\n- documentation file: \"0511-UL-V109-23\", role: documentation_file, no date/pages.\n\nrevision_history: revision \"-\", date 2024-11-11, changes \"Issuing certificate\". Schema says revision string e.g. \"0\" not 0. \"-\" → \"-\". Keep \"-\".\n\nfootnotes: marker \"1\" (shown as ¹), text: \"The ratio Q3 / Q1 shall be chosen according to paragraph 4.1.4 of OIML R 49-1:2013\".\n\nWhich values reference footnote 1? The Q3/Q1 cell \"≤ 1000 1\" — that trailing \"1\" likely is the footnote marker. And power supply fields (battery type, umax, umin, frequency, software version). I'll add footnote_markers [\"1\"] to: available ratio constraint (type-level q3_q1 note?), power_supply_type, power_supply_voltage_max, power_supply_voltage_min, software_version, crc? The CRC row doesn't show marker in markdown (\"CRC checksum (of legally relevant SW):\" no ¹). Software version row header \"Software version¹\" has it. I'll add markers to power_supply_type, power_supply_voltage_max, power_supply_voltage_min, software_version.\n\nAlso the type-level Q3/Q1 row: \"≤ 1000 1 for Q3=2.5m3/h; ≤ 630 1 for Q3=1.6m3/h\" — I'm capturing per-model ratios via tables; the type-level constraint is captured in config_level max_ratio entry with footnote markers [\"1\"].\n\ncertificate: scheme \"A\", number \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.06\", project_number — not given; omit? The \"CZ1\" part is country/authority code, not project. Omit project_number. page_total: 7. member_state: \"Czech Republic\". date_issued: \"2024-11-11\". oiml_issuer_id: not present — omit. Recommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [2]. Wait — R49 accuracy class 2 (metrological class). Yes: \"For accuracy class (if applicable): 2\".\n\nrecommendation.id canonical \"R49\". edition: 2013 (int). amendment: none — omit.\n\ncertificate.model_family: family_name: \"Smartio\". models: 56 entries. label maybe \"DN15 Q3=1.6 R=40 ultrasonic water meter\" — keep label simple like \"Smartio DN15 Q3=1.6 R=40\". I'll just include model_id and label same-ish; maybe omit label to save space. Schema allows omit.\n\nLet me decide model_id format: \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R=40\"? I'll use \"Smartio DN15 Q3=1.6 R=40\". Consistent across model_level and matrix rows? Matrix rows use fields, not ids. Good.\n\nOrdering: DN15 first (R40..R1000 interleaved by Q3), then DN20.\n\nDN15 models (28):\n1. DN15 Q3=1.6 R=40\n2. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=40\n3. DN15 Q3=1.6 R=50\n4. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=50\n5. DN15 Q3=1.6 R=63\n6. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=63\n7. DN15 Q3=1.6 R=80\n8. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=80\n9. DN15 Q3=1.6 R=100\n10. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=100\n11. DN15 Q3=1.6 R=125\n12. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=125\n13. DN15 Q3=1.6 R=160\n14. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=160\n15. DN15 Q3=1.6 R=200\n16. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=200\n17. DN15 Q3=1.6 R=250\n18. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=250\n19. DN15 Q3=1.6 R=315\n20. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=315\n21. DN15 Q3=1.6 R=400\n22. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=400\n23. DN15 Q3=1.6 R=500\n24. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=500\n25. DN15 Q3=1.6 R=630\n26. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=630\n27. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=800\n28. DN15 Q3=2.5 R=1000\n\nDN20 models (28):\n29. DN20 Q3=2.5 R=40\n30. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=40\n31. DN20 Q3=2.5 R=50\n32. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=50\n33. DN20 Q3=2.5 R=63\n34. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=63\n35. DN20 Q3=2.5 R=80\n36. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=80\n37. DN20 Q3=2.5 R=100\n38. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=100\n39. DN20 Q3=2.5 R=125\n40. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=125\n41. DN20 Q3=2.5 R=160\n42. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=160\n43. DN20 Q3=2.5 R=200\n44. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=200\n45. DN20 Q3=2.5 R=250\n46. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=250\n47. DN20 Q3=2.5 R=315\n48. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=315\n49. DN20 Q3=2.5 R=400\n50. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=400\n51. DN20 Q3=2.5 R=500\n52. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=500\n53. DN20 Q3=2.5 R=630\n54. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=630\n55. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=800\n56. DN20 Q3=4.0 R=1000\n\nCheck DN15 table3 row3: Q3/Q1 row: \"630\" colspan 2, then \"800\", then \"1000\". Q1 row: 0.0025, 0.004, 0.003, 0.0025. Q3: 1.600, 2.500, 2.500, 2.500. So col1: R630 Q3=1.6 (q1 0.0025); col2: R630 Q3=2.5 (q1 0.004); col3: R800 Q3=2.5 (q1 0.003); col4: R1000 Q3=2.5 (q1 0.0025). ✓ matches my list.\n\nDN20 table3: Q1: 0.004, 0.006, 0.005, 0.004; Q3: 2.500, 4.00, 4.00, 4.00; ratios 630(×2), 800, 1000. col1: R630 Q3=2.5 q1=0.004; col2: R630 Q3=4.0 q1=0.006; col3: R800 Q3=4.0 q1=0.005; col4: R1000 Q3=4.0 q1=0.004. ✓.\n\nQ2 values: DN15 table1: 0.064, 0.100, 0.051, 0.080, 0.041, 0.064, 0.032, 0.050, 0.026, 0.040, 0.021, 0.032. Map: R40/1.6→0.064; R40/2.5→0.100; R50/1.6→0.051; R50/2.5→0.080; R63/1.6→0.041; R63/2.5→0.064; R80/1.6→0.032; R80/2.5→0.050; R100/1.6→0.026; R100/2.5→0.040; R125/1.6→0.021; R125/2.5→0.032. ✓\n\nTable2 Q2: 0.016, 0.025, 0.013, 0.020, 0.010, 0.016, 0.008, 0.013, 0.006, 0.010, 0.005, 0.008 → R160/1.6=0.016, R160/2.5=0.025, R200/1.6=0.013, R200/2.5=0.020, R250/1.6=0.010, R250/2.5=0.016, R315/1.6=0.008, R315/2.5=0.013, R400/1.6=0.006, R400/2.5=0.010, R500/1.6=0.005, R500/2.5=0.008. ✓\n\nTable3 Q2: 0.004 (R630/1.6), 0.006 (R630/2.5), 0.005 (R800/2.5), 0.004 (R1000/2.5). ✓\n\nDN20 table1 Q1: 0.063, 0.100, 0.050, 0.080, 0.040, 0.064, 0.031, 0.050, 0.025, 0.040, 0.020, 0.032 → R40/2.5=0.063, R40/4.0=0.100, R50/2.5=0.050, R50/4.0=0.080, R63/2.5=0.040, R63/4.0=0.064, R80/2.5=0.031, R80/4.0=0.050, R100/2.5=0.025, R100/4.0=0.040, R125/2.5=0.020, R125/4.0=0.032. ✓\nQ2: 0.100, 0.160, 0.080, 0.128, 0.064, 0.102, 0.050, 0.080, 0.040, 0.064, 0.032, 0.051. ✓ (R63/4.0 → 0.102.)\nQ3: 2.500, 4.00 alternating; Q4: 3.125, 5.00.\n\nDN20 table2 Q1: 0.016, 0.025, 0.013, 0.020, 0.010, 0.016, 0.008, 0.013, 0.006, 0.010, 0.005, 0.008 → R160/2.5=0.016, R160/4.0=0.025, R200/2.5=0.013, R200/4.0=0.020, R250/2.5=0.010, R250/4.0=0.016, R315/2.5=0.008, R315/4.0=0.013, R400/2.5=0.006, R400/4.0=0.010, R500/2.5=0.005, R500/4.0=0.008. ✓\nQ2: 0.025, 0.040, 0.020, 0.032, 0.016, 0.026, 0.013, 0.020, 0.010, 0.016, 0.008, 0.013. ✓\n\nDN20 table3: as above: R630/2.5: q1 0.004, q2 0.006; R630/4.0: q1 0.006, q2 0.010; R800/4.0: q1 0.005, q2 0.008; R1000/4.0: q1 0.004, q2 0.006. ✓\n\nNow, is \"components[]\" applicable? The water meter consists of body, transducers, electronic indicating device — but these are integral, not separately certified sub-assemblies with type designations. The impulse module is NOT part of certificate. I'll skip components. Module designation \"-\" → \"Not applicable\".\n\ncertified_type.description: summarize the description section (English only). I'll include a condensed version:\n\"The water meters type Smartio are ultrasonic water meters with an electronic indicating device, designed to measure, memorise and display the volume at metering conditions of water passing through the measurement transducer. They consist of a cast brass body with connecting screw threads, one pair of ultrasonic transducers and the electronic indicating device (LCD display showing volume and flow). Volume resolution of 0.00001 m³ on the digital display. Without buttons; NFC sensor for non-legally relevant communication. Software separation; SW versions and CRCs displayed in the auto-rounding menu. Displays separately delivered volume for reverse and forward flow every two minutes; permanently shown volume is the difference. May be equipped with an impulse module which is not part of this certificate. Powered by 3.6 V battery. Can be installed in any position.\"\n\ntype_level characteristics — finalize list:\n- nominal_diameter: hmm this varies by model → model_level (values 15, 20) or config axis. I'll include in model_level per model... Actually simpler: include \"nominal_diameter\" as model_level attribute with two distinct values across 56 models. Yes.\n- q2_q1_ratio: 1.6 (dimensionless)\n- q3_q4_ratio: 1.25\n- measuring_principle: \"ultrasonic\"\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- maximum_permissible_error_lower_flowrate_zone_mpel: 5, unit \"%\", value sign ±. I'll do value 5, unit \"%\" — maybe better to capture \"±\" in attribute name? \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_flowrate_zone\" with value 5 unit \"%\" is standard. OK.\n- maximum_permissible_error_upper_flowrate_zone_mpeu: 2, \"%\"\n- temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_class: \"MAP 16\" (attribute max_admissible_pressure_class)\n- pressure_loss_class: config_level by nominal_diameter (see above)\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: 50 °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- indicating_range_testing_mode: 9999 m³\n- indicating_range_user_mode: 999999 m³\n- resolution_indicating_device_testing_mode: 0.00001 m³\n- resolution_indicating_device_user_mode: 0.001 m³\n- resolution_device_rapid_testing: 100 pulse/dm³\n- resolution_indicating_device_rapid_testing: 0.000001 m³\n- eut_testing_category: string\n- eut_case: \"B\"\n- connection_type: config_level by nominal_diameter\n- minimum_straight_length_inlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- flow_conditioner_required: false (value \"No\"? Use value: false? Shape allows number|string|object. Use string \"No\"? Hmm, boolean not allowed strictly. I'll use value: false — risky per shape. Rule says value: <number | {min,max} | string>. Use \"No\".)\n- mounting: omit (\"-\")\n- orientation: \"any\" (same as orientation_limitation; keep one: orientation \"any\")\n- length: {min: 105, max: null} mm\n- reverse_flow: \"Designed to measure\" → maybe \"measures reverse flow\": keep string \"Designed to measure\".\n- mechanical_environment_class: \"B, O\" → string. Or [\"B\",\"O\"]? Use \"B, O\".\n- electromagnetic_environment_class: \"E2\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min:-25, max:70} °C\n- power_supply_type: \"Non replaceable battery\" [footnote 1]\n- power_supply_voltage_max: 3.6 V [1]\n- power_supply_voltage_min: 1.9 V [1]\n- power_supply_frequency: omit (\"-\" = none, battery powered)\n- minimum_battery_lifetime: 16 \"years\" — hmm value 16, unit \"years\"? Wait the label says [years] and cell \"16 years\". value 16, unit \"a\"? Use unit_symbol \"years\".\n- software_version: \"0.6.28, 1.0.1\" [1]\n- crc_checksum: \"0x854EBACF, 0x13372073, 0x3A681C19, 0x1d901C40\"\n- software_type: \"P (embedded, built-for-purpose)\" hmm. I'll do software_category: \"embedded software, type P\". Maybe skip? It's meaningful for WELMEC software doc. Include: software_requirements: \"Specific requirements for embedded software for built-for-purpose measuring instrument (type P)\". And software_extensions: \"I1 (Water meters), S (Software separation), D (Download of Legally Relevant Software)\".\n\nunit_id: leave null everywhere unless certain. I'll omit unit_id or set null. Schema shows unit_id in the shape; I'll include with null only where relevant? The strict shape includes unit_id. Including \"unit_id\": null everywhere adds tokens. The shape says every value MUST be object with value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers. I'll include all keys but keep footnote_markers only when present? \"footnote_markers\": [\"1)\"] shown in shape. For compactness, include footnote_markers only when non-empty; include unit_id: null... Hmm strictness vs size. The instruction: \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\". I'll include unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers in every object (footnote_markers [] when none? or omit). To reduce size on 336 model_level entries, maybe omit unit_id and footnote_markers when null/empty. I think the grader is lenient; but \"STRICT\" says must have that shape. Compromise: include \"unit_id\": null always? For 336+ entries, that's +336×~14 tokens ≈ 4700 tokens. Heavy.\n\nAlternative: reduce model_level duplication — since matrix_tables contain all data, maybe model_level only needs nominal_diameter... No, rules explicitly want model_level populated. Let me just comply fully but smartly: for model_level entries I'll include value objects with \"value\" and \"unit_symbol\" at the attribute level (schema shows model_level: {attribute, unit_symbol, values:[{model, value}]}). Note the R137 example: model_level entries have unit_symbol at attribute level and values as {\"model\", \"value\"} — NOT structured objects per value! Look at the example:\n\n```json\n\"model_level\": [\n  {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}, {\"model\": \"G2.5\", \"value\": 4.0}]}\n]\n```\n\nYes! In model_level, unit_symbol is at attribute level, values are plain {model, value}. Great — that saves a lot. So per schema:\n\n```\nmodel_level[]:\n  attribute, unit, values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}\n```\n\nSo values entries: {model, value} and optionally footnote_markers. \n\nFor type_level, use the full structured shape {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}.\n\nFor config_level: {attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value, unit_symbol}} — per rule 5 structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol}. I'll follow that.\n\nFor matrix_tables rows: plain numbers per column, with column_units array. Good.\n\nNow write the JSON. Structure:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.06\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"page_total\": 7,\n    \"member_state\": \"Czech Republic\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2024-11-11\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Czech Metrology Institute\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"Jan Kalandra\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [{ \"name\": \"JANZ - Contagem e Gestao de Fluídos, S.A.\", \"address_lines\": [\"Avenida Infante D. Henrique, 288\", \"1950-421 Lisboa\", \"Portugal\"] }],\n  \"manufacturers\": [ same ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"Smartio\"],\n    \"module_designation\": \"Not applicable\",\n    \"description\": \"...\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"Smartio\",\n    \"models\": [56 entries]\n  },\n  \"components\": [] — omit entirely (rule: include ONLY if named sub-assemblies; none). Omit.\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {...},\n    \"model_level\": [...],\n    \"config_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [6 tables],\n  \"recommendation\": {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [2]},\n  \"test_reports\": [\n    {\"id\": \"0511-ER-V109-23\", \"date\": \"2024-11-11\", \"pages\": 25, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n    {\"id\": \"6015-PT-P5013-24\", \"pages\": 201, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n    {\"id\": \"0511-UL-V109-23\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n  ],\n  \"revision_history\": [{\"revision\": \"-\", \"date\": \"2024-11-11\", \"changes\": \"Issuing certificate\"}],\n  \"footnotes\": [{\"marker\": \"1\", \"text\": \"The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen according to paragraph 4.1.4 of OIML R 49-1:2013\"}]\n}\n\nAddress note: the applicant name cell included \"Obergrundstrasse 119\" — likely an artifact from another layout; PT address given. I'll ignore Obergrundstrasse (it's a Swiss street, inconsistent with Lisboa PT address — clearly an artifact of the form template). Keep name clean.\n\nDate format: \"2024-11-11\".\n\nNow model_level attributes:\n- nominal_diameter: unit \"mm\"? DN15 is DN nominal in mm. unit_symbol \"mm\". values 15/20.\n- q1: m³/h\n- q2: m³/h\n- q3: m³/h\n- q4: m³/h\n- ratio_q3_q1: dimensionless (unit null)\n\nEach 56 values. Order consistent with models list.\n\nLet me write out all values.\n\nDN15 Q3=1.6 series (q1, q2, q3, q4):\nR40: 0.040→0.04, 0.064, 1.6, 2.0\nR50: 0.032, 0.051, 1.6, 2.0\nR63: 0.025, 0.041, 1.6, 2.0\nR80: 0.020→0.02, 0.032, 1.6, 2.0\nR100: 0.016, 0.026, 1.6, 2.0\nR125: 0.013, 0.021, 1.6, 2.0\nR160: 0.010→0.01, 0.016, 1.6, 2.0\nR200: 0.008, 0.013, 1.6, 2.0\nR250: 0.006, 0.010→0.01, 1.6, 2.0\nR315: 0.005, 0.008, 1.6, 2.0\nR400: 0.004, 0.006, 1.6, 2.0\nR500: 0.003, 0.005, 1.6, 2.0\nR630: 0.0025, 0.004, 1.6, 2.0\n\nDN15 Q3=2.5 series:\nR40: 0.063, 0.1, 2.5, 3.125\nR50: 0.05, 0.08, 2.5, 3.125\nR63: 0.04, 0.064, 2.5, 3.125\nR80: 0.031, 0.05, 2.5, 3.125\nR100: 0.025, 0.04, 2.5, 3.125\nR125: 0.02, 0.032, 2.5, 3.125\nR160: 0.016, 0.025, 2.5, 3.125\nR200: 0.013, 0.02, 2.5, 3.125\nR250: 0.01, 0.016, 2.5, 3.125\nR315: 0.008, 0.013, 2.5, 3.125\nR400: 0.006, 0.01, 2.5, 3.125\nR500: 0.005, 0.008, 2.5, 3.125\nR630: 0.004, 0.006, 2.5, 3.125\nR800: 0.003, 0.005, 2.5, 3.125\nR1000: 0.0025, 0.004, 2.5, 3.125\n\nDN20 Q3=2.5:\nR40: 0.063, 0.1, 2.5, 3.125\nR50: 0.05, 0.08, 2.5, 3.125\nR63: 0.04, 0.064, 2.5, 3.125\nR80: 0.031, 0.05, 2.5, 3.125\nR100: 0.025, 0.04, 2.5, 3.125\nR125: 0.02, 0.032, 2.5, 3.125\nR160: 0.016, 0.025, 2.5, 3.125\nR200: 0.013, 0.02, 2.5, 3.125\nR250: 0.01, 0.016, 2.5, 3.125\nR315: 0.008, 0.013, 2.5, 3.125\nR400: 0.006, 0.01, 2.5, 3.125\nR500: 0.005, 0.008, 2.5, 3.125\nR630: 0.004, 0.006, 2.5, 3.125\n\nDN20 Q3=4.0:\nR40: 0.1, 0.16, 4, 5\nR50: 0.08, 0.128, 4, 5\nR63: 0.064, 0.102, 4, 5\nR80: 0.05, 0.08, 4, 5\nR100: 0.04, 0.064, 4, 5\nR125: 0.032, 0.051, 4, 5\nR160: 0.025, 0.04, 4, 5\nR200: 0.02, 0.032, 4, 5\nR250: 0.016, 0.026, 4, 5\nR315: 0.013, 0.02, 4, 5\nR400: 0.01, 0.016, 4, 5\nR500: 0.008, 0.013, 4, 5\nR630: 0.006, 0.01, 4, 5\nR800: 0.005, 0.008, 4, 5\nR1000: 0.004, 0.006, 4, 5\n\nOK. Now matrix tables. Columns: nominal_diameter, q3_q1_ratio, q1, q2, q3, q4. Units: [mm, null, m³/h, m³/h, m³/h, m³/h].\n\nSix tables:\n1. name \"Basic metrological data (flowrates) — DN15, ratio 40–125\": rows 12\n2. \"… DN15, ratio 160–500\": rows 12\n3. \"… DN15, ratio 630–1000\": rows 4\n4. \"… DN20, ratio 40–125\": rows 12\n5. \"… DN20, ratio 160–500\": rows 12\n6. \"… DN20, ratio 630–1000\": rows 4\n\nDescription: \"Flowrate tables from pages 5–6; each column pair corresponds to one certified variant (Q3/Q1 ratio), DN15 tables 1–3 and DN20 tables 4–6.\"\n\nRow objects: {\"nominal_diameter\": 15, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 40, \"q1\": 0.04, \"q2\": 0.064, \"q3\": 1.6, \"q4\": 2.0}\n\nWait, for DN15 table 1, the interleaving: columns are [R40 Q3=1.6, R40 Q3=2.5, R50 Q3=1.6, ...]. So rows alternate Q3. I'll list rows in column order: (40,1.6),(40,2.5),(50,1.6),(50,2.5),...\n\nConfig_level entries:\n1. pressure_loss_class, axis \"nominal_diameter\": values: [{condition: \"DN15\", value: \"Q3 1.6: Δp 10; Q3 2.5: Δp 16\", unit_symbol: null}, {condition: \"DN20\", value: \"Q3 2.5: Δp 16; Q3 4.0: Δp 25\"}]. Structured shape {condition, value, unit_symbol}. value string.\n2. connection_type, axis \"nominal_diameter\": DN15: \"NPSM or G type 3/4\\\", 7/8\\\", 1\\\"\" ; DN20: \"NPSM or G type 7/8\\\", 1\\\", 1 1/4\\\"\".\n3. max available ratio? add: attribute \"maximum_ratio_q3_q1\", axis \"nominal_diameter_and_q3\": conditions \"DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h\" → 630; \"DN15, Q3=2.5 m³/h\" → 1000; \"DN20, Q3=2.5 m³/h\" → 630; \"DN20, Q3=4.0 m³/h\" → 1000. With footnote marker 1? The markers appeared in that cell. Values entries {condition, value, unit_symbol, footnote_markers:[\"1\"]}? Rule 5 says values follow same structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol}. I'll add footnote_markers where applicable.\n\nType-level q3_q1 ratio marking: The general table lists \"Q3/Q1: ≤1000¹ for Q3=2.5 (DN15)... \" — I'm